// ModuleInit — the two synthesized functions a program with MANAGED top-level `var`s carries, and
// nothing else does: `__module_init`, which fills every EAGER managed global's `.data` pointer slot BEFORE
// `main`, and `__maxon_global_cleanup`, which releases each managed global AFTER `main` returns.
//
// ⚠ **"EAGER" IS DOING WORK IN THAT SENTENCE** (spec-port `lazy-static`): a `static` member's slot is
// filled on FIRST ACCESS by its own `__lazy_init#<label>`, which this file also builds. `__module_init`
// skips those; the cleanup does NOT, because a lazy slot still has to be released — it merely has to be
// null-guarded first (`dropLazyGlobalSlot`).
//
// ⭐ **A MANAGED GLOBAL'S SLOT IS AN 8-BYTE POINTER, AND ITS VALUE IS A REAL OWNED HEAP RECORD.** Both
// halves are load-bearing and neither is negotiable:
//
//   • **A pointer, because a record cannot be baked.** A `.data` slot holding the ADDRESS of another
//     section's bytes would need a compile-time VA, and there is none under ASLR
//     (`specs/static-variables.md:729-730`). So the image holds zero and this function writes the address.
//
//     ⚠⚠ **SO EVERY SLOT ACCESS IS ONE WIDTH — `declaredAggregateSlotWidth()` — AND IT IS STATED RATHER
//     THAN DERIVED FROM THE VALUE'S TYPE.** The store that fills a slot and the load that drains it pass
//     that accessor and not `global.valueType`, which is a fact about the value's IDENTITY and not about
//     the slot. Deriving it happened to give the machine word for every arm that existed — every managed
//     `MaxonType` collapses to `i64` — and a boxed union is where the two part company, loudly:
//     `maxonTypeToStdType` panics on a `named` outright (*"must be resolved to a primitive before
//     lowering"*), that tag being the value's identity and never its storage. It is the same
//     identity-vs-storage split `payloadStorageOf` states for a union PAYLOAD slot, reached through the
//     same shared accessor so the two widths cannot come apart.
//   • **OWNED, not the immortal `.rdata` record a `let` shares.** A `var` is MUTATED — `msg.append("!")`
//     rewrites `buffer@0`/`length@8` in place — and mutating the one shared record would make every `let`
//     of the same literal observe the change, while dropping it would free read-only data. The spec
//     leak-gates exactly this (`static-variables.md`'s `top-level-var-string-mutate-cross-function`: "must
//     NOT be shared as an immortal static record: it mutates correctly and frees cleanly"), and the
//     runnable oracle enforces the same boundary from the other side — its static-literal path EXCLUDES a
//     mutable global (`MaxonToStandardConversion.cs:1045-1049`, "a mutable global array stays heap and COWs
//     on first write").
//
// ⭐⭐ **THEY ARE BUILT AT THE MAXON TIER, NOT THE STD TIER — the one design choice worth stating.** Every
// other compiler-synthesized function in shv2 (`installMmRuntime`, `installStringRuntime`, the destructor
// cascades) is built as already-lowered `StdOp`s and appended AFTER the pipeline. These two are not,
// because what they have to build is a LITERAL'S RECORD, and `LowerMaxonToStd` already knows how to build
// one from a single op: `stringLiteral` becomes the rdata blob + record + fixup, and `constArrayLiteral`
// becomes the ten-op owned-`Array`-over-immortal-blob sequence. Emitting Maxon ops costs six op
// constructions and reuses both; emitting Std ops would be a SECOND copy of `lowerConstArrayLiteral`,
// which is the duplication this compiler pays for hardest. So the price is the one thing a Maxon-tier
// function has to carry that a Std-tier one does not — its dense per-value type columns — and
// `SynthesizedFunction` below is that price, paid once.
//
// ⚠ **THE ENTRY STUB CALLS THEM, AND THE ENTRY STUB IS BUILT AFTER DEAD-FUNCTION ELIMINATION.** So they
// are ROOTED unconditionally in `DeadFunctionElimination.seedRoots` — the same treatment, for the same
// reason, as `__mm_leak_check`: nothing in the module calls them, and a pruned root leaves the stub's
// `call`/`bl`/`call $n` fixup with no target (loud on every backend, never silent).

// ⭐ **WHICH TAGS CARRY A NAME THAT WAS INTERNED SOMEWHERE — the question `projectScopedNameId` crosses on.**
// A primitive names nothing, and a `genericInstance` carries a whole-program `GenericInstanceId` rather than
// a name id (there is exactly ONE `GenericInstanceRegistry` in the compiler), so neither needs a crossing;
// these four are the tags whose id is an index into a TYPE-NAME table, and therefore table-relative.
function tagNamesAnIndexInternedType(tag ValueTypeTag) returns bool
	return tag == ValueTypeTag.named or tagIsStructRef(tag) or tag == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef or tag == ValueTypeTag.function
end 'tagNamesAnIndexInternedType'

// The two synthesized names. `__`-prefixed, so `isCompilerInternalCallee` treats them as the compiler's
// own (no E3004, no signature slotting) and `TargetPrinter.isRuntimeFunction` keeps them out of every
// golden fragment — a program with no managed global emits neither, so the corpus is unmoved.
//
// The spellings are BOTH reference compilers': the bootstrap emits `__module_init` (2-Parser.cs:2436) and
// `__maxon_global_cleanup` (MaxonToStandardConversion.cs:2739), and v1 emits `__module_init_<n>` per file.
// Keeping the names identical costs nothing and makes a dump of one compiler's output readable against
// the other's.
// ⭐ **THE TWO SENTINEL VALUES A LAZY STATIC'S SLOT CAN HOLD BESIDES A RECORD** (spec-port `lazy-static`).
//
// `UninitializedSlot` is the zero the `.data` image already puts there — the SAME number
// `ProgramSignatures.nullPointerSlot` writes, named here from the reader's side so the guard states which
// value it is testing for rather than spelling a bare `0`. `InitializingSlot` is written for the duration of
// the initializer and is the whole of the cycle detection. Neither is an address any allocator returns, so
// the two and every real record are disjoint by construction.
let UninitializedSlot = 0 as ParsedInt
let InitializingSlot = 1 as ParsedInt

export let ModuleInitName = b"__module_init"
export let GlobalCleanupName = b"__maxon_global_cleanup"

// `__maxon_global_cleanup`'s single parameter — `main`'s ExitCode — occupying the ValueId the parameter
// numbering reserves for argument 0. See `buildGlobalCleanup` for why the function takes and returns it.
let ExitCodeParam = 0 as ValueId

// ⭐ **BUILD BOTH FUNCTIONS INTO `module`, AND ANSWER WHETHER THE PROGRAM HAS ANY.** The single bit every
// entry stub reads (`RuntimeUsage.usesManagedGlobals`), decided HERE — where the arena is walked — rather
// than re-derived downstream by probing the module for a function name. That is the same discipline
// `BackendDispatch`'s header states for the GT/leak gates, and for the reason it states: dead-function
// elimination can take an installed function back out, so "is it still there?" is not the same question as
// "was it installed?".
//
// A program with no managed global builds NOTHING — no functions, no ops, no `.data` — which is what keeps
// this rung byte-neutral for the entire existing corpus.
export function installManagedGlobalInit(module MaxonModule, project Project, globals ManagedGlobalArray) returns bool
	if globals.count() == 0 'noManagedGlobals'
		return false
	end 'noManagedGlobals'

	buildModuleInit(module, project: project, globals: globals)
	// ⭐⭐ **ONE ROUTINE PER LAZY STATIC** (spec-port `lazy-static`), built whether or not this program's
	// `__module_init` has anything to do: the two populations are disjoint and either can be empty.
	for global in globals 'eachGlobal'
		if global.filledOnFirstAccess 'onFirstAccess'
			buildLazyStaticInit(module, project: project, global: global)
		end 'onFirstAccess'
	end 'eachGlobal'
	buildGlobalCleanup(module, project: project, globals: globals)
	return true
end 'installManagedGlobalInit'

// ⭐⭐ **`__lazy_init$<label>()` — A STATIC FIELD'S INITIALIZER, RUN ON FIRST ACCESS** (spec-port
// `lazy-static`). `specs/lazy-static.md`: *"The initializer expression is evaluated the first time the static
// field is accessed"*, and *"After initialization, subsequent accesses return the cached value"*. Every
// access calls this (`Parser.emitGlobalAddr`); this decides whether anything happens.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE GUARD IS THE SLOT ITSELF — AN IN-BAND SENTINEL, NOT A SEPARATE FLAG — AND THE REASON IS THAT A
// FLAG AND A VALUE CAN DISAGREE.** The `.data` image starts the slot at zero and a built record is a heap
// address, so `slot == 0` already means "not yet initialized" with no second word to keep in step. That is
// not merely economical, it is what makes the `static var` REASSIGNMENT case correct for free: `State.set(s)`
// stores a fresh non-null record through a completely different code path (`Parser.emitCheckedGlobalStore`),
// and because the guard IS the value, that path cannot leave the guard unset and cannot cause the
// initializer to re-run. A separate flag would have required that path to write the flag too — a second
// write, in another file, that nothing would force anyone to remember.
//
// ⭐ **AND A SECOND SENTINEL CATCHES THE CYCLE.** `InitializingSlot` is written before the initializer runs
// and overwritten by the record, so a static whose initializer transitively reads ITSELF meets it and
// panics by name. That is strictly better than either reference: MEASURED, the bootstrap re-enters, reads
// the still-null slot and dies in `mm_incref called with NULL pointer` with no mention of the cycle. Address
// 1 is not a record any allocator returns, so the two sentinels and every real value are disjoint by
// construction.
//
// ⚠ **WHY A CYCLE IS REACHABLE AT ALL, WHERE IT WAS NOT BEFORE.** The rule that a managed initializer may
// not NAME another global (`ProgramSignatures.evaluateDecl` answers `notFound`) still holds and is unchanged
// — but it bounds the initializer EXPRESSION, not the body of a function that expression calls. Under eager
// init that hole gave a silent wrong answer (a body reading a later global read the null the image left,
// which `buildModuleInit`'s header already recorded); on demand it gives unbounded recursion instead, so it
// needs an answer rather than a note.
function buildLazyStaticInit(module MaxonModule, project Project, global ManagedGlobal)
	// The body is the user's factory call tree, so this frame's depth is the program's: it keeps the
	// green-thread stack guard the rest of the compiler's scaffolding is exempt from. See
	// `TargetPrinter.skipsGreenThreadStackGuard`.
	var fn = SynthesizedFunction.open(module, project: project, name: global.lazyInitCallee(), returnType: MaxonReturnType.void, paramTypes: MaxonTypeArray.create(), needsGreenThreadStackGuard: true)
	let range = global.range

	let entry = fn.currentBlock()
	let guardWord = emitSlotGuardWord(fn, global: global)
	let isUninitialized = emitSlotIs(fn, word: guardWord.word, sentinel: UninitializedSlot, range: range)

	let build = fn.openBlock(b"build")
	// The in-progress mark goes down BEFORE the initializer runs — that ordering is the whole of the cycle
	// detection, and it is why the store is not merged with the one below it.
	fn.emit(MaxonOp.storeIndirect(guardWord.addr, offset: GlobalSlotOffset, value: fn.emitTypedLiteral(InitializingSlot, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range), valueType: MaxonType.integer), range: range)
	let record = initialRecordOf(fn, global: global)
	fn.emit(MaxonOp.storeIndirect(guardWord.addr, offset: GlobalSlotOffset, value: record, valueType: declaredAggregateSlotWidth()), range: range)
	fn.terminate(MaxonOp.retVoid, range: range)

	let settled = fn.openBlock(b"settled")
	let isInitializing = emitSlotIs(fn, word: guardWord.word, sentinel: InitializingSlot, range: range)

	let cycle = fn.openBlock(b"cycle")
	fn.emit(MaxonOp.panicAbort(lazyStaticCycleMessage(global)), range: range)
	// `panicAbort` diverges, and a block still needs a terminator — the dead return `closeFunctionBody`
	// emits after any diverging op, for its reason.
	fn.terminate(MaxonOp.retVoid, range: range)

	let ready = fn.openBlock(b"ready")
	fn.terminate(MaxonOp.retVoid, range: range)

	fn.terminateBlock(entry, op: MaxonOp.condBranch(isUninitialized, thenBlockId: build.id, elseBlockId: settled.id), range: range)
	fn.terminateBlock(settled, op: MaxonOp.condBranch(isInitializing, thenBlockId: cycle.id, elseBlockId: ready.id), range: range)
end 'buildLazyStaticInit'

// The panic an in-progress access reports. It names the SLOT rather than a route, because the slot is the
// one thing every party has in common and the only one this routine can see.
//
// ⛔ **IT USED TO SAY "its initializer reads the static it is initializing", FULL STOP, AND THAT
// MISDIAGNOSED A PROGRAM THAT IS NOT CYCLIC AT ALL** (§6 review finding 9). `InitializingSlot` is equally
// observable when a green thread PARKS inside an initializer (any IO or `await` in a callee parks it) and a
// SECOND thread then first-accesses the same static. Both are "accessed while initializing"; only one is a
// cycle. The sentence now names the condition and offers the cycle as the usual cause rather than the only
// one. ⚠ shv2 has no per-static initialization LOCK, so the second thread panics rather than waiting — that
// is a real limit of this design, recorded here because the message is where an author meets it.
function lazyStaticCycleMessage(global ManagedGlobal) returns ByteArray
	var message = b"lazy static initializer for `".clone()
	message.append(global.label)
	message.append(b"` was accessed while its own initializer was still running, so it has no value to answer with. Usually the initializer reads the static it is initializing, directly or through a function it calls. It is also reachable across GREEN THREADS: if the initializer parks (any IO or `await` in a callee) and a second thread then first-accesses the same static, that access arrives here too")
	// The line terminator every panic message carries, so the stack trace that follows starts on its own
	// line — `RuntimeAbort.panicMessageLine` appends the same constant for a message that HAS a source
	// position to prefix, which a synthesized routine has not.
	message.append(PanicMessageLineTerminator)
	return message
end 'lazyStaticCycleMessage'

// `__module_init()` — for each managed global, in ARENA order: build its initial owned record and store
// the record's address into its `.data` slot.
//
// ⚠ **THE STORE IS BARE — NO LOAD OF THE OLD VALUE AND NO DECREF.** The slot is guaranteed zero here (the
// `.data` image put it there and nothing has run yet), so a decref would be reading a null pointer. Both
// reference compilers reach the same place by a different road: their initializing store is on the SAME
// path as an ordinary assignment, so they emit the load-and-decref and then suppress it by matching the
// enclosing FUNCTION'S NAME (`isModuleInit = func.Name == "__module_init"`,
// MaxonToStandardConversion.cs:2327). shv2 has no such match because it has no such shared path: the
// user-visible re-assignment lives in `Parser.emitCheckedGlobalStore` and this initialization lives here,
// so the fact ("this store is the first one") is expressed by WHICH CODE EMITS IT rather than by a string
// compare in the middle of a lowering.
//
// ⚠ **ARENA ORDER IS DETERMINISTIC AND CORRECTNESS DOES NOT DEPEND ON IT.** A managed initializer may not
// reference another global — `evaluateDecl` answers `notFound` for a managed decl, so `let A = "x"` /
// `let B = A` is E2004 in BOTH declaration orders (see `ProgramSignatures.evaluateDecl`, and the oracle
// measured to agree) — so there is no initialization order to get wrong. The order here is fixed only so
// the emitted code is a function of the program: the arena is source-path order, then in-file order.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A LAZY SLOT IS NOT IN IT** (spec-port `lazy-static`) — canonical says a static field's initializer
// runs on FIRST ACCESS, so it gets `__lazy_init#<label>` instead and this function never touches its slot.
// That is the entire behavioural difference between the two, and it is one `continue` over the decided bit
// (`ManagedGlobal.filledOnFirstAccess`, never a second reading of where the binding was declared).
function buildModuleInit(module MaxonModule, project Project, globals ManagedGlobalArray)
	var fn = SynthesizedFunction.open(module, project: project, name: ModuleInitName, returnType: MaxonReturnType.void, paramTypes: MaxonTypeArray.create())

	for global in globals 'eachGlobal'
		if global.filledOnFirstAccess 'onFirstAccess'
			continue
		end 'onFirstAccess'

		let record = initialRecordOf(fn, global: global)
		fn.emit(MaxonOp.storeIndirect(fn.emitSlotAddr(global), offset: GlobalSlotOffset, value: record, valueType: declaredAggregateSlotWidth()), range: global.range)
	end 'eachGlobal'

	fn.terminate(MaxonOp.retVoid, range: anchorRange(globals))
end 'buildModuleInit'

// The OWNED value a managed global's slot starts life holding — either a record this function MATERIALIZES,
// or whatever a user factory RETURNS.
//
// ⭐ **BOTH ARMS CAN RUN THE PROGRAM'S OWN CODE** — the factory call outright (spec-port
// `map-struct-bytearray`), and, since W41, a container record whose `create` is a DECLARED generic's static
// rather than a runtime entry. That is exactly what `DeadGlobalElimination`'s header filed against the day an
// initializer may call.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE KEEP-ALIVE THIS HEADER USED TO ASSERT NOW APPLIES TO ONE OF THIS FUNCTION'S TWO CALLERS ONLY**
// (spec-port `lazy-static`, §6 review finding 6). It said a global whose initializer calls is kept alive by
// `contributesStorage` "so this run is never spliced out from under its side effects". That is still true for
// `buildModuleInit`, whose globals run BEFORE `main` whether or not anything reads them. It is deliberately
// FALSE for `buildLazyStaticInit`, which now shares this producer: `contributesStorage` asks
// `declScopeInitializerCall`, which SILENCES the keep-alive for a lazy slot — because canonical says a static
// field nothing accesses must run nothing, so preserving its side effects would preserve the very effects the
// language forbids. A lazy static's liveness is decided by whether an access exists, which is the same thing
// as whether its initializer can run at all.
function initialRecordOf(fn SynthesizedFunction, global ManagedGlobal) returns ValueId
	return match global.initializer 'initializer'
		record(build) gives builtRecord(fn, build: build, valueType: global.valueType, range: global.range)
		factoryCall(calls, root) gives initializerNodeRecord(fn, calls: calls, id: root, valueType: global.valueType, range: global.range)
	end 'initializer'
end 'initialRecordOf'

// ⭐ **ONE RECORD, MATERIALIZED — the shared producer for a global's own slot AND for a factory argument.**
// The three kinds differ in exactly one thing — how much of the record the literal's own materialization
// already is — and that difference is a fact about the record formats, not a choice made here:
//
//   • a **String** literal is a WHOLLY immortal `.rdata` record (`lowerStringLiteral`), shared by every
//     read of that literal in the program, so it is CLONED into a fresh owned heap String. `__str_clone`
//     is the `(box) -> newBox` dual of the `__str_decref` the cleanup drops it with, so the pair balances
//     by construction.
//   • a **constant array** literal — a `b"…"` byte string, or any all-constant `[…]` whose element stores
//     its own bytes — ALREADY materializes as an owned heap 48-byte `Array` record over an immortal
//     `.rdata` blob (`lowerConstArrayLiteral`), which is precisely what the slot must hold — so there is
//     nothing to clone. Its first receiver-writing method detaches the buffer off `.rdata`
//     (`byte-string-literal.push-grows-off-rdata`), which is what makes a MUTABLE one work.
//   • a **container** with no constant image has no literal record at all (P1.7 slice 2b): the record is
//     BUILT here, exactly as `Parser`'s body emitters build one — this instance's `create` with the stamps
//     it fixes, then one `__managed_push` per folded element.
//
// ⚠ **A FOURTH ARM, A `CharacterSet` PRESET, WENT AT W129** with the `__cs_*` runtime it sealed its member set
// into. It was the container arm plus one `__cs_make`; `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon` declares the eleven presets
// now (W115), so a preset at module scope is an ordinary LAZY STATIC calling ordinary declared code, and there
// is no compiler-owned record left for a global to hold.
function builtRecord(fn SynthesizedFunction, build ManagedGlobalRecord, valueType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	return match build 'build'
		stringRecord(bytes) gives clonedStringRecord(fn, bytes: bytes, valueType: valueType, range: range)
		constArrayRecord(elementSize, elementCount, bytes) gives ownedConstArrayRecord(fn, elementSize: elementSize, elementCount: elementCount, bytes: bytes, valueType: valueType, range: range)
		containerRecord(create, elements) gives builtContainerRecord(fn, create: create, elements: elements, valueType: valueType, range: range)
		boxedUnionCaseRecord(tag, payloadSlotOffsets, boxSize) gives builtUnionCaseBox(fn, tag: tag, payloadSlotOffsets: payloadSlotOffsets, boxSize: boxSize, valueType: valueType, range: range)
	end 'build'
end 'builtRecord'

// The String arm: materialize the immortal `.rdata` literal, then CLONE it into a fresh owned heap
// String the slot can own, mutate and eventually free. Shared with the array build, whose STRING ELEMENTS
// need the identical promotion for the identical reason — the array's `element_destroy@40` walk drops every
// live slot, so an element must be a record its owner may free — which is why it takes the type and bytes
// rather than reading them off a global.
function clonedStringRecord(fn SynthesizedFunction, bytes ByteArray, valueType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	let literal = fn.mintOfType(valueType)
	fn.emit(MaxonOp.stringLiteral(literal, bytes: bytes), range: range)
	return fn.emitPositionalCall(StrCloneName, args: singletonArgs(literal), resultTag: maxonTypeTag(valueType), resultNameId: typeNameIdOf(valueType), range: range)
end 'clonedStringRecord'

// ⭐⭐ **A BOXED UNION'S PAYLOAD-FREE CASE AT MODULE SCOPE — THE BOX, THE TAG, THEN THE ZEROES.** The same
// shape `Parser.emitEnumBox` emits inside a function for the same construct written at file scope, minus the
// half a payload-free case does not have: there is no argument to type-check, nothing to move in, and so no
// ownership to transfer. Every layout fact was resolved where the layout lives
// (`ProgramSignatures.boxedUnionRecordOf`), so nothing here reads a registry — `builtStructBox`'s discipline.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE ZERO-FILL IS NOT REDUNDANT WITH `__mm_alloc`'s ZEROED SLAB, AND `emitEnumBox` SAYS SO IN AS MANY
// WORDS.** It pins a deterministic 0 in the unused slots, which is exactly what the destructor cascade's
// null-guard skips — so a zeroed managed slot is never decref'd as a bogus pointer — and `emitEnumBox`'s own
// note records that the slab's zeroing carries a "a future free-list must re-zero" caveat this store does
// not. A global's box and a body's box of one case must be the same bytes: ONE `__destruct_<U>` drops both.
//
// ⚠ **THE BOX IS NOT ENROLLED AS AN OWNED TEMPORARY**, for the reason `SynthesizedFunction.emitBoxAlloc`
// states: `__module_init` has no statement to hang a drop on, and this record is stored straight into a
// `.data` slot whose owner releases it through `__maxon_global_cleanup`.
function builtUnionCaseBox(fn SynthesizedFunction, tag ParsedInt, payloadSlotOffsets ByteOffsetArray, boxSize ByteOffset, valueType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	let box = fn.emitBoxAlloc(boxSize as ParsedInt, resultTag: maxonTypeTag(valueType), resultNameId: typeNameIdOf(valueType), range: range)
	fn.emit(MaxonOp.storeIndirect(box, offset: UnionTagOffset, value: fn.emitTypedLiteral(tag, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range), valueType: MaxonType.integer), range: range)
	for offset in payloadSlotOffsets 'zeroFill'
		fn.emit(MaxonOp.storeIndirect(box, offset: offset, value: fn.emitTypedLiteral(0 as ParsedInt, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range), valueType: MaxonType.integer), range: range)
	end 'zeroFill'

	return box
end 'builtUnionCaseBox'

// The constant-array arm: ONE op, because the literal's own materialization is already the owned heap
// record the slot must hold. The stride and the element count ride the description rather than being
// re-derived here — a `b"…"` fixes them from its bytes, an `[…]` from the instance it was typed by, and
// neither is a fact this function could re-ask without picking one of the two to be authoritative.
function ownedConstArrayRecord(fn SynthesizedFunction, elementSize ElementStride, elementCount ParsedInt, bytes ByteArray, valueType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	let record = fn.mintOfType(valueType)
	fn.emit(MaxonOp.constArrayLiteral(record, elementSize: elementSize, elementCount: elementCount, bytes: bytes), range: range)
	return record
end 'ownedConstArrayRecord'

// ⭐ **A CONTAINER GLOBAL'S RECORD IS BUILT AT RUN TIME** (P1.7 slice 2b, every container since spec-port
// `map-struct-bytearray`) — the ops `Parser`'s body emitters make for `[10, 20, 30]` or `MapAlias.create()`
// inside a function, emitted here for the same thing written at file scope. Deliberately NOT v1's
// `ConstantArrayLiteralRdata` (`Compiler/Passes/`), which moves an all-constant-integer element BUFFER into
// `.rdata` while still heap-allocating the header: that is an OPTIMIZATION over this, it is the optimizer's
// call to make, and building it now would fork a second element-emission path before the first one is proven.
//
// ⚠ **THE CREATE AND ITS STAMPS ARE THE INSTANCE'S, NOT THIS FUNCTION'S.** Both were resolved where the
// instance registry lives (`ProgramSignatures.containerCreateCall`), off the very instance the global's slot
// is typed by — so the strides this record is born with and the type every use loads through cannot
// disagree, and each column's destructor equals the one a body's `create()` of the same instance stamps.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A DECLARED GENERIC'S `create()` ARRIVES AS THAT SAME DESCRIPTION WITH AN EMPTY STAMP LIST (W41)**, so
// the loop below emits nothing and the call is a bare `Map.create()`. Its HIDDEN arguments — the layout
// descriptor and one witness per `where` constraint — are not this function's to supply and deliberately are
// not in the description: `LowerMaxonToStd.finalizeCallArgsWithLayout` appends them, sourcing both from the
// call's RESULT for a `Self`-returning static (`witnessSourceValue`), and the result is minted at
// `genericInstance(id)` by `resultTag`/`resultNameId` below. That is why the emitted call is byte-identical to
// the one the same `StrMap.create()` lowers to inside a function, and why building the block here instead
// would have been a second, driftable copy of an ABI.
//
// It takes the type and range rather than the `ManagedGlobal` for its two siblings' reason, and now the
// three arms read alike: nothing here is a fact about a GLOBAL, and a builder that cannot see one cannot
// come to read a second field off it that the other two arms would not have — which is also what lets all
// three serve a factory ARGUMENT unchanged.
function builtContainerRecord(fn SynthesizedFunction, create ContainerCreateCall, elements ConstArrayElementArray, valueType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	let record = bornContainerRecord(fn, create: create, valueType: valueType, range: range)

	// ⭐ **THE ADD CALLEE IS THE INSTANCE'S OWN** (spec-port `lazy-static`) — `__managed_push` for an `Array`,
	// `Set.insert` for a declared `Set`. It used to be spelled here, which was true while an `Array` was the only
	// container a top-level form could hand elements to; `ProgramSignatures.containerCreateCall` is where
	// "which container is this?" is already decided, so it answers this too rather than a second test living
	// in the synthesis.
	if elements.count() > 0 'hasElements'
		let add = elementAddCallee(create, count: elements.count())
		for element in elements 'eachElement'
			_ = fn.emitPositionalCall(add, args: pairArgs(record, second: elementRecordOf(fn, element: element, range: range)), resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
		end 'eachElement'
	end 'hasElements'

	return record
end 'builtContainerRecord'

// The EMPTY record a container description is born as — the `create` callee with its stamps, and nothing
// else. Split out when the map literal arrived, because the two container builders differ ONLY in how an
// element joins: a one-column container pushes a value, a map upserts a labelled pair, and every fact about
// the BIRTH — which callee, which stamps, which tag the result is minted at — is one answer both read.
//
// ⚠ **A SECOND CALL STOOD IN THIS SEQUENCE — `__managed_resize`, SIZING A FIXED-SIZE CONTAINER'S RECORD
// (W190).** A vector was the one container whose elements are addressable from birth, and its global record
// was born through the runtime `__managed_create` and then grown. `stdlib/Vector.maxon`'s own `create()`
// publishes those slots now, so the sizing rides inside the callee this function emits and the description
// carries no `sizing` field at all.
function bornContainerRecord(fn SynthesizedFunction, create ContainerCreateCall, valueType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	var args = ValueIdArray.create()
	for stamp in create.stamps 'eachStamp'
		args.push(containerStampValue(fn, stamp: stamp, range: range))
	end 'eachStamp'
	return fn.emitPositionalCall(create.callee, args: args, resultTag: maxonTypeTag(valueType), resultNameId: typeNameIdOf(valueType), range: range)
end 'bornContainerRecord'

// ⭐⭐ **`var keywordMap = [b"if": KeywordInfo.create(…), …]` — A MAP LITERAL AT FILE SCOPE**, emitted as the
// two calls a body's literal emits: `Map.create()` then one `Map.upsert(key, value: v)` per pair
// (`Parser.parseMapLiteralBody`). One desugar written at two positions, so a literal-built map is one
// representation wherever it was written.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS AN ARENA NODE AND NOT A `ManagedGlobalRecord`, WHICH IS WHY IT TAKES `calls`.** Either half of a
// pair may itself be a CALL — `KeywordInfo.create(TokenKind.if, helpText: …)` is the whole point of the
// shape — and only an arena entry can name one. `materializedValue` recurses back through
// `initializerNodeRecord`, exactly as a factory ARGUMENT does.
function builtMapLiteralRecord(fn SynthesizedFunction, create ContainerCreateCall, entries GlobalInitMapEntryArray, calls GlobalInitCallArray, valueType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	let record = bornContainerRecord(fn, create: create, valueType: valueType, range: range)
	if entries.count() > 0 'hasPairs'
		let add = keyedElementAddCallee(create, count: entries.count())
		for entry in entries 'eachPair'
			emitMapEntryUpsert(fn, add: add, record: record, entry: entry, calls: calls, range: range)
		end 'eachPair'
	end 'hasPairs'

	return record
end 'builtMapLiteralRecord'

// ⭐ **ONE PAIR INTO THE MAP.** The key is materialized before the value — a body's left-to-right order — and
// each half is released after the call only if the CALL kept nothing, through the very `dropBorrowedArguments`
// a factory's arguments go through. ⚠ For `Map.upsert` it keeps BOTH — the table pushes them into its own
// column arrays, which is a type-parameter FEED and not a consume bit — so this emits no drop at all, and the
// one reference this frame made becomes the table's (`ProgramSignatures.calleeKeepsArgumentAtAFeed`, whose
// header carries the use-after-free measured without it). The LABELS are the resolution's
// (`ProgramSignatures.globalMapEntry`): the receiver is positional, the key is positional and the value
// carries `value:`, which is what `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerCall` slots the third argument against.
function emitMapEntryUpsert(fn SynthesizedFunction, add ByteArray, record ValueId, entry GlobalInitMapEntry, calls GlobalInitCallArray, range SourceRange)
	let key = materializedValue(fn, value: entry.key.value, valueType: entry.key.valueType, calls: calls, range: range)
	let value = materializedValue(fn, value: entry.value.value, valueType: entry.value.valueType, calls: calls, range: range)

	var labels = ByteArrayArray.create()
	labels.push(EmptySourceText)
	labels.push(entry.key.label)
	labels.push(entry.value.label)
	_ = fn.emitCall(add, args: tripleArgs(record, second: key, third: value), argLabels: labels, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)

	var borrowed = GlobalInitArgumentArray.create()
	borrowed.push(entry.key)
	borrowed.push(entry.value)
	dropBorrowedArguments(fn, args: borrowed, values: pairArgs(key, second: value), range: range)
end 'emitMapEntryUpsert'

// The callee one element joins this container through — asked only when there IS an element, so a container
// that names none (a `List`, a declared generic's bare `create()`) is not required to have one. ⚠ A `Map` no
// longer belongs on that list: since the top-level map literal it names a KEYED add, which is the arm below. A
// description that arrives holding elements and naming no callee is an INVARIANT BREAK, never a case: it
// means an initializer form was opened without teaching the router what its container adds through, and
// dropping the elements silently is the wrong answer to that.
function elementAddCallee(create ContainerCreateCall, count ParsedInt) returns ByteArray
	return match create.elementAdd 'add'
		push(callee) gives callee
		unsupported panic("ModuleInit.elementAddCallee: `{String.from(create.callee)}` was handed {count} element(s) and `ProgramSignatures.containerCreateCall` names no add callee for it — a top-level form is producing elements for a container the router does not know how to fill")
		// A KEYED add takes `(record, key, value:)`, and this caller holds ONE value per element — so passing
		// it here would put a key where a value belongs, silently. `builtMapLiteralRecord` is the builder that
		// has a pair to give; a one-column element list reaching a keyed container is a description built by
		// one form and emitted by the other.
		upsert panic("ModuleInit.elementAddCallee: `{String.from(create.callee)}` was handed {count} single-column element(s) and its add is KEYED — a one-column literal cannot fill a container whose add takes a `(key, value)` pair")
	end 'add'
end 'elementAddCallee'

// The KEYED twin of the callee lookup above — asked only when there IS a pair, for its reason, and refusing
// the one-column arms for the mirror of it: a pair cannot be handed to an add that takes a single value.
function keyedElementAddCallee(create ContainerCreateCall, count ParsedInt) returns ByteArray
	return match create.elementAdd 'add'
		upsert(callee) gives callee
		push to unsupported panic("ModuleInit.keyedElementAddCallee: `{String.from(create.callee)}` was handed {count} `(key, value)` pair(s) and `ProgramSignatures.containerCreateCall` names no keyed add for it — a map literal is filling a container the router does not know is keyed")
	end 'add'
end 'keyedElementAddCallee'

// One stamp a container's `create` is born holding: a number, or the drop callee's raw `.text` ADDRESS. The
// emitting twin of `Parser.columnDestroyValue`, over the ONE decision both read
// (`ProgramSignatures.columnDestroyStamp`). The address rides a `functionRef`, which
// `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerFunctionRef` materializes BARE for a `__`-prefixed callee (not the `__fnref_` env
// thunk — the drop walk invokes it through a plain `callIndirect(ptr)`) and `scanRuntimeUsage` records, so
// naming it here is what installs the destructor.
function containerStampValue(fn SynthesizedFunction, stamp ContainerCreateStamp, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	return match stamp 'stamp'
		inline(value) gives fn.emitTypedLiteral(value, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		dropCallee(callee) gives fn.emitRuntimeFuncAddr(callee, range: range)
	end 'stamp'
end 'containerStampValue'

// ⭐⭐ **THE THREE WAYS AN ARENA NODE PRODUCES ITS RECORD — the program's own code RETURNS one, the compiler
// BUILDS the box itself, or a CONTAINER is created and then filled** (spec-port `map-struct-bytearray`; W116;
// the top-level map literal).
//
// ⭐⭐ **AN ARGUMENT OR A FIELD MAY BE ANOTHER NODE, AND THEN THIS IS THE RECURSION** (spec-port
// `module-level-struct-var`) — `var state = Outer.create(Inner.create(0))`, `static var base = Outer{inner:
// Inner.create(7)}`. `id` names the entry to emit in the initializer's flat arena; a nested value names
// another entry, and materializing it comes back here. The emitted order is exactly a body's: depth-first,
// left to right, so an inner node — and the drops of ITS borrowed arguments — is complete before the node
// that receives it is made.
//
// `valueType` is supplied by whoever RECEIVES the result — the global's slot for the root, the argument or
// field for a nested one — which is why no arena entry carries a return type of its own, and why both arms
// mint at it identically.
function initializerNodeRecord(fn SynthesizedFunction, calls GlobalInitCallArray, id InitializerCallId, valueType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	return match globalInitCallAt(calls, id: id) 'node'
		call(callee, args) gives calledFactoryRecord(fn, callee: callee, args: args, calls: calls, valueType: valueType, range: range)
		construct(sizeBytes, fields) gives builtStructBox(fn, sizeBytes: sizeBytes, fields: fields, calls: calls, valueType: valueType, range: range)
		mapLiteral(create, entries) gives builtMapLiteralRecord(fn, create: create, entries: entries, calls: calls, valueType: valueType, range: range)
	end 'node'
end 'initializerNodeRecord'

// ⭐⭐ **THE SLOT'S OCCUPANT, RETURNED BY THE PROGRAM'S OWN CODE** (spec-port `map-struct-bytearray`) —
// `var db = Database.create(EntryMap.create(), sourcePaths: StringArray.create())`. Each argument is
// materialized first, in SOURCE order, and then one ordinary `MaxonOp.call`: the same op a body's call site
// emits, carrying the same labels, so `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerCall` slots the labelled arguments against the
// callee's declared parameter names exactly as it does everywhere else. Nothing about the call is special
// here, which is the point — the factory is an ordinary function and this is an ordinary call to it.
//
// ⚠ **THE RESULT IS OWNED AND NOTHING ELSE HOLDS IT.** A user factory hands back a fresh record (or one it
// has already taken ownership of through its consumed parameters), the slot becomes its sole owner, and
// `__maxon_global_cleanup` drops it through the callee's return type's own drop — the same pair every other
// arm balances by.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE ARGUMENTS THE CALLEE ONLY BORROWED ARE STILL THIS FUNCTION'S.** They are materialized here, so
// this frame owns them; a body's call site hands that job to the statement's own temp list
// (`Parser.trackOwnedTemp`) and `__module_init` has no statement to end. So each one is dropped right after
// the call, on the decision the index already made per argument (`GlobalInitArgumentCleanup`).
function calledFactoryRecord(fn SynthesizedFunction, callee ByteArray, args GlobalInitArgumentArray, calls GlobalInitCallArray, valueType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	var values = ValueIdArray.create()
	var labels = ByteArrayArray.create()
	for arg in args 'eachArgument'
		values.push(materializedValue(fn, value: arg.value, valueType: arg.valueType, calls: calls, range: range))
		labels.push(arg.label)
	end 'eachArgument'

	let record = fn.emitCall(callee, args: values, argLabels: labels, resultTag: maxonTypeTag(valueType), resultNameId: typeNameIdOf(valueType), range: range)
	dropBorrowedArguments(fn, args: args, values: values, range: range)
	return record
end 'calledFactoryRecord'

// ⭐⭐ **`Pair{a: 1, b: 2}` AT MODULE SCOPE — THE BOX, THEN ONE STORE PER SLOT (W116)** — the same shape
// `Parser.emitStructLiteral` emits inside a function, for the same construct written in a `static` field's
// initializer. Every layout fact it needs was resolved where the layout lives
// (`ProgramSignatures.globalStructConstruct`), so nothing here reads a registry.
//
// ⚠⚠ **A MANAGED FIELD IS CONSUMED BY THE BOX, AND THERE IS NO DROP AFTER THE STORES — WHICH IS THE
// OPPOSITE OF THE CALL ARM ABOVE.** Every producer `materializedValue` reaches hands back a value this frame
// OWNS: a String literal arrives already CLONED off its immortal `.rdata` record (`clonedStringRecord`), a
// container is freshly built, and a nested node's result is fresh. Storing it makes the box its sole owner
// and `__destruct_<Struct>` releases exactly one reference per managed slot — so a drop here would be the
// second half of a double free, exactly as `dropBorrowedArguments` would be if a callee had CONSUMED its
// argument. That is why `GlobalStructField` carries no cleanup bit to get wrong.
//
// ⚠ The stores go down in DECLARATION order because `fields` is layout-parallel, which is the ordering
// `emitStructLiteral` also stores in and deliberately not the order the literal was written
// (`ConstFactoryCall.construct`).
function builtStructBox(fn SynthesizedFunction, sizeBytes ByteOffset, fields GlobalStructFieldArray, calls GlobalInitCallArray, valueType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	let box = fn.emitBoxAlloc(sizeBytes as ParsedInt, resultTag: maxonTypeTag(valueType), resultNameId: typeNameIdOf(valueType), range: range)
	for field in fields 'eachField'
		let value = materializedValue(fn, value: field.value, valueType: field.valueType, calls: calls, range: range)
		fn.emit(MaxonOp.storeIndirect(box, offset: field.offset, value: value, valueType: field.storageType), range: range)
	end 'eachField'

	return box
end 'builtStructBox'

// Release every argument record the callee did not take. AFTER the call, never before: the callee reads
// through the borrow, so the record has to outlive the body it was lent to.
function dropBorrowedArguments(fn SynthesizedFunction, args GlobalInitArgumentArray, values ValueIdArray, range SourceRange)
	for i in 0 upto args.count() 'eachArgument'
		let arg = try args.get(i) otherwise panic("dropBorrowedArguments: args.get OOB at {i} below count {args.count()}")
		let value = try values.get(i) otherwise panic("dropBorrowedArguments: values.get OOB at {i} — the materialization above pushes one value per argument")
		match arg.cleanup 'cleanup'
			none then continue
			dropAfterCall(dropCallee) then fn.emitPositionalCallDiscarding(dropCallee, args: singletonArgs(value), range: range)
		end 'cleanup'
	end 'eachArgument'
end 'dropBorrowedArguments'

// ONE constant, materialized. A scalar is a `literal` at its own folded type; a record is built through the
// very producer a global's own slot goes through; and a nested NODE is produced through the very producer the
// enclosing one is — which is what makes "an argument is anything an initializer may be" a fact about the
// emitted code and not only about the grammar.
//
// ⭐ **AN ARGUMENT AND A STRUCT FIELD COME THROUGH HERE ALIKE (W116)**, which is what makes `Outer{inner:
// Inner.create(7)}` fall out of the arena rather than needing an emitter of its own. It takes the value and
// its type rather than the enclosing `GlobalInitArgument` for exactly that reason: a field carries no label
// and no cleanup, and a producer that could read them would be a producer only one caller could use.
//
// ⚠ **EVERY ARM HANDS BACK A VALUE THIS FRAME OWNS.** What differs is who ENDS that ownership, and it is
// decided by the caller: an argument the callee only borrowed is dropped after the call
// (`ProgramSignatures.recordArgumentCleanup`), a field is consumed by the box and dropped by its destructor
// (`builtStructBox`). Nothing downstream of here has to know which arm produced the value.
function materializedValue(fn SynthesizedFunction, value GlobalInitArgumentValue, valueType MaxonType, calls GlobalInitCallArray, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	return match value 'value'
		scalar(payload) gives fn.emitTypedLiteral(payload, valueType: valueType, range: range)
		record(build) gives builtRecord(fn, build: build, valueType: valueType, range: range)
		call(id) gives initializerNodeRecord(fn, calls: calls, id: id, valueType: valueType, range: range)
	end 'value'
end 'materializedValue'

// ⭐ **THE TYPE A SCALAR LITERAL'S OP CARRIES — ITS STORAGE, WHICH IS NOT ITS IDENTITY.** Every scalar type
// is its own storage except a `named` one: a payload-free enum case and a ranged alias are both an i64, and
// the tier below has no `named` to lower. See `emitTypedLiteral` for the two ways the missing distinction
// showed up.
function literalStorageType(valueType MaxonType) returns MaxonType
	return MaxonType.integer if maxonTypeTag(valueType) == ValueTypeTag.named else valueType
end 'literalStorageType'

// ONE element's value, ready to be pushed. An integer element is its number; a String element is the
// immortal `.rdata` literal CLONED, because the array becomes the element's sole owner and `__managed_decref`'s
// walk `__str_decref`s every live slot — the same promotion `Parser.moveElementIntoContainer` applies to a
// borrowed String literal at a body's `push`, reached through the same `__str_clone`.
function elementRecordOf(fn SynthesizedFunction, element ConstArrayElement, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	return match element 'element'
		integer(payload) gives fn.emitTypedLiteral(payload, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
		string(bytes) gives clonedStringRecord(fn, bytes: bytes, valueType: MaxonType.string, range: range)
		// A Character element is the String arm at a different TAG, which is the whole of the difference in
		// shv2: `Parser.emitCharacterLiteral` emits the same `stringLiteral` op and the same `__str_clone`
		// promotes it, and the tag is what makes the value a `Character` for the set's key check.
		character(bytes) gives clonedStringRecord(fn, bytes: bytes, valueType: MaxonType.character, range: range)
		// ⭐ **AN ENUM CASE IS THE INTEGER ARM, and it is written out rather than folded into it because the
		// two bind different shapes.** A payload-free enum's value IS its tag, so the slot takes the same i64
		// the integer arm stores — and it is typed `integer` here for the same reason `emitEnumTagLiteral`
		// types its op that way: the ELEMENT TYPE is the array instance's business (`Array with Reg`, fixed by
		// `constArrayValueType`), and this is the STORAGE. The enum's name is carried on the element for the
		// instance to be built from, and is not wanted here.
		enumCase(payload, _) gives fn.emitTypedLiteral(payload, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range)
	end 'element'
end 'elementRecordOf'

// `__maxon_global_cleanup(exitCode) returns exitCode` — one decref per managed global, after `main`.
//
// ⭐ **IT TAKES AND RETURNS `main`'s EXIT CODE, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS SHAPED LIKE `__mm_leak_check`.** The
// call sits on the entry stub's exit path, between `main` and the leak gate, where the ONE live value is
// the exit code — in R8 on x64, x0 on arm64, on the operand stack on wasm. A void function would clobber
// it on x64 (a Maxon function returns in R8) and force each stub to invent a save/restore; a pass-through
// is what `__mm_leak_check` already does, so the stubs gain one call in an existing shape rather than a
// new one. The code is not read here — it flows through untouched.
//
// ⚠⚠ **A FILE-SCOPE SLOT'S DECREF IS UNCONDITIONAL AND A LAZY ONE'S IS NULL-GUARDED, AND THE HEADER THAT
// USED TO STAND HERE SAID THE GUARD COULD NEVER BE NEEDED.** It read: *"Both reference compilers guard it
// (`EmitDecrefValueIfNonnull`; v1's `__mm_decref_maybenull_helper`), because in their designs a global whose
// initializer is LAZY, or whose file never ran an init, can still be null at exit. shv2 has neither case."*
// **shv2 now has the first case** (spec-port `lazy-static`): a `static` member nothing ever accessed reaches
// this function with its slot still zero, and an unguarded `__str_decref(null)` on it is a crash on exit for
// a program that did nothing wrong. So the two references' guard was not an artefact of their designs — it
// was a consequence of laziness, and shv2 inherits it exactly where it inherits the laziness.
//
// ⭐ The file-scope half is UNCHANGED and stays unguarded, on the argument the old header made and which is
// still true of it: `__module_init` fills every eager entry before `main`, so a branch there would test a
// condition the program cannot be in.
function buildGlobalCleanup(module MaxonModule, project Project, globals ManagedGlobalArray)
	var paramTypes = MaxonTypeArray.create()
	paramTypes.push(MaxonType.integer)
	var fn = SynthesizedFunction.open(module, project: project, name: GlobalCleanupName, returnType: MaxonReturnType.value(MaxonType.integer), paramTypes: paramTypes)

	for global in globals 'eachGlobal'
		if global.filledOnFirstAccess 'mayBeUnfilled'
			dropLazyGlobalSlot(fn, global: global)
			continue
		end 'mayBeUnfilled'

		dropGlobalSlot(fn, global: global)
	end 'eachGlobal'

	fn.terminate(MaxonOp.ret(ExitCodeParam), range: anchorRange(globals))
end 'buildGlobalCleanup'

// One eager slot's drop: load and decref, no guard. The shared body of both arms above, so the two cannot
// come to load the slot differently.
function dropGlobalSlot(fn SynthesizedFunction, global ManagedGlobal)
	let record = fn.mintOfType(global.valueType)
	fn.emit(MaxonOp.loadIndirect(record, addr: fn.emitSlotAddr(global), offset: GlobalSlotOffset, valueType: declaredAggregateSlotWidth()), range: global.range)
	fn.emitPositionalCallDiscarding(global.dropCallee, args: singletonArgs(record), range: global.range)
end 'dropGlobalSlot'

// ⭐ **A LAZY SLOT IS DROPPED ONLY IF IT HOLDS A RECORD — i.e. IF IT IS NEITHER SENTINEL.**
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT TESTED ONLY `!= UninitializedSlot`, AND THE §6 REVIEW (finding 2) WAS RIGHT TO REFUSE THE
// ARGUMENT THAT DEFENDED IT.** That header claimed `InitializingSlot` was unreachable here because cleanup
// runs after `main` returned, so any initializer that started had finished. Two ways for the word to still
// be the in-progress mark: a green thread PARKED inside an initializer when `main` returns (any IO or
// `await` in a callee parks it), and — were a throwing factory ever admitted — an unwind between the two
// stores. Testing for both sentinels costs one compare and one block, and it DELETES the argument rather
// than defending it: there is no longer a reachability claim to get wrong.
//
// ✅ **A THROWING FACTORY IS NOT ADMISSIBLE TODAY, AND THE REFUSAL IS INCIDENTAL RATHER THAN DESIGNED**
// (MEASURED, §6 review's open question). `Parser.requireFactoryReturnsARecord` classifies the RETURN TYPE
// only and says nothing about `throws` — but the body this file synthesizes emits an ordinary
// `MaxonOp.call`, never a `tryCall`, and `SemanticCheck.validatePlainCall` walks synthesized functions like
// any other: a callee in `throwsMap` reached by a plain call is **E3057 `throwing function requires try`**.
// Verified on `static var b = Box.make()` with a throwing `make()`. ⚠ So the second hole above is closed by
// a rule that is not about global initializers at all and could move without anyone noticing here — which
// is the second reason this guard tests the mark rather than reasoning about who could have left it.
//
// ⚠ **AND IT STAYS A TEST FOR THE SENTINELS RATHER THAN `> InitializingSlot`.** The two happen to be the
// two smallest words, so an ordered compare would work today — and would silently re-encode the
// disjointness `UninitializedSlot`'s header argues as an ORDERING fact instead.
function dropLazyGlobalSlot(fn SynthesizedFunction, global ManagedGlobal)
	let range = global.range
	let guardWord = emitSlotGuardWord(fn, global: global)
	let isUninitialized = emitSlotIs(fn, word: guardWord.word, sentinel: UninitializedSlot, range: range)

	let head = fn.currentBlock()
	let settled = fn.openBlock(b"settled")
	let isInitializing = emitSlotIs(fn, word: guardWord.word, sentinel: InitializingSlot, range: range)

	let drop = fn.openBlock(b"drop")
	dropGlobalSlot(fn, global: global)

	let after = fn.openBlock(b"after")
	fn.terminateBlock(head, op: MaxonOp.condBranch(isUninitialized, thenBlockId: after.id, elseBlockId: settled.id), range: range)
	fn.terminateBlock(settled, op: MaxonOp.condBranch(isInitializing, thenBlockId: after.id, elseBlockId: drop.id), range: range)
	fn.terminateBlock(drop, op: MaxonOp.branch(after.id), range: range)
	fn.resumeBlock(after)
end 'dropLazyGlobalSlot'

// ⭐ **THE SLOT'S ADDRESS AND ITS GUARD WORD, READ ONCE** — the pair `buildLazyStaticInit` and
// `dropLazyGlobalSlot` both open with, and the pair that must CHANGE TOGETHER whenever the sentinel
// vocabulary does. The §6 review's finding 8: it was written out twice in this file, and finding 2 was
// exactly a change one copy needed and the other had. The slot's declared type is the record's; this reads
// it as a WORD, because every test over it is about the eight bytes rather than about what they point at —
// the same reading `emitSlotAddr` gives an address.
function emitSlotGuardWord(fn SynthesizedFunction, global ManagedGlobal) returns SlotGuardWord
	let addr = fn.emitSlotAddr(global)
	let word = fn.mintOfType(MaxonType.integer)
	fn.emit(MaxonOp.loadIndirect(word, addr: addr, offset: GlobalSlotOffset, valueType: MaxonType.integer), range: global.range)
	return SlotGuardWord.create(addr, word: word)
end 'emitSlotGuardWord'

// Is the guard word exactly `sentinel`? Both readers test both sentinels, so the comparison is stated once
// rather than four times with the literal and the operator respelled at each.
function emitSlotIs(fn SynthesizedFunction, word ValueId, sentinel ParsedInt, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
	return fn.emitCompare(word, rhs: fn.emitTypedLiteral(sentinel, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range), op: MaxonCmpOp.eq, range: range)
end 'emitSlotIs'

// The slot ADDRESS and the WORD read out of it — returned together because every caller needs both, and
// re-deriving the address would emit a second `globalAddr` for one slot.
type SlotGuardWord
	export let addr as ValueId
	export let word as ValueId

	static function create(addr ValueId, word ValueId) returns SlotGuardWord
		return Self{addr: addr, word: word}
	end 'create'
end 'SlotGuardWord'

// The span a synthesized function's TERMINATOR carries. No source token spawns a `return` that the user
// did not write, so it rides the FIRST global's initializer — the declaration that caused the whole
// function to exist. The caller has already established the list is non-empty (`installManagedGlobalInit`
// builds nothing for an empty one), so the miss is an invariant break rather than a case.
function anchorRange(globals ManagedGlobalArray) returns SourceRange
	return (try globals.get(0) otherwise panic("anchorRange: the managed-global list is empty — `installManagedGlobalInit` returns before building a function when it is")).range
end 'anchorRange'

// ⭐ **A MAXON FUNCTION BUILT BY THE COMPILER RATHER THAN PARSED** — the emit + value-minting discipline
// `FileParseArtifact` gives the parser, for the one caller that has no artifact.
//
// **IT EXISTS FOR THE DENSE TYPE COLUMNS.** `LowerMaxonToStd` reads a value's tag out of
// `IrFunction.valueTags` for every op it lowers (`valueTagOf` PANICS on a value with no entry) and its
// name id out of `valueNameIds`, and it seeds `nextValueId` from `valueTags.count()` — so a ValueId
// allocated without an entry in all four columns would shift every later value's type by one. Minting
// through one door is what makes that impossible, exactly as `Parser.mintValue` does for parsed code.
//
// It records into `project.opRanges` and `project.valueOrigins` in the same lockstep
// `FileParseArtifact.emitOp` uses — the span table is keyed by op index and asserts it is appended in
// step, so a synthesized op that skipped it would misattribute every later span by one.
// ⭐ EXPORTED since SV1 wave 3, whose `Runtime/ServiceLoop.maxon` is the second producer of Maxon-tier
// functions the compiler builds — the FIRST outside this file. Everything the carrier's header says is why
// it exists holds for that producer word for word: a `<T>.__loop` needs the dense per-value type columns
// `LowerMaxonToStd` reads, and a second carrier would be a second place they could fall out of lockstep.
export type SynthesizedFunction
	var module as MaxonModule
	var project as Project
	// This function's position in `module.functions` — the key `ValueOriginTable` files an origin under.
	var funcIndex as FuncIndex
	// The function itself, so a second block can be minted into it. Held BESIDE the index rather than looked
	// up from it because `MaxonModule` exposes no by-index accessor, and adding one to serve a single caller
	// would be a wider API than the need.
	var func as IrFunction
	var block as IrBlock
	var valueTags as ValueTypeTagArray
	var valueNameIds as TypeNameIdArray
	var valueBrandIds as TypeNameIdArray
	var valueClosureEnv as ValueIdArray
	var valueIsCapturingClosure as BoolArray

	// Create the function, register it and its single `entry` block on `module`, and seed the value
	// columns with the parameters — whose ids (`0..paramCount-1`) the value numbering RESERVES rather than
	// mints, exactly as `Parser.recordValueType` does for a parsed function's parameters.
	export static function open(module MaxonModule, project Project, name ByteArray, returnType MaxonReturnType, paramTypes MaxonTypeArray, needsGreenThreadStackGuard bool = false) returns SynthesizedFunction
		var valueTags = ValueTypeTagArray.create()
		var valueNameIds = TypeNameIdArray.create()
		var valueBrandIds = TypeNameIdArray.create()
		var valueClosureEnv = ValueIdArray.create()
		var valueIsCapturingClosure = BoolArray.create()
		for paramType in paramTypes 'eachParam'
			valueTags.push(maxonTypeTag(paramType))
			valueNameIds.push(typeNameIdOf(paramType))
			valueBrandIds.push(UnnamedTypeId)
			valueClosureEnv.push(0)
			valueIsCapturingClosure.push(false)
		end 'eachParam'

		// ⚠ `synthesized()` is a POSITIVE claim, not a default: it is what makes `__module_init` /
		// `__maxon_global_cleanup` read as the compiler's own at `FunctionNameIndex.refuseDuplicateFunctionName`
		// (so a `Builtins.maxon` declaration of one is E4015 against them, and a doubled install is a panic), at
		// `DeadFunctionElimination.markCompilerOwnedRoot` (E4015 again, for a program that installs neither and
		// so has no pair to count) and at `TargetPrinter.isRuntimeFunction`. ⚠ The Maxon-tier population is
		// `__module_init`, `__maxon_global_cleanup` and one `__lazy_init#<label>` per lazy static — the last of
		// which is the one that carries `needsGreenThreadStackGuard`.
		var func = IrFunction.create(name, maxonReturnType: returnType, paramCount: paramTypes.count(), maxonParamTypes: paramTypes, valueTags: valueTags, valueNameIds: valueNameIds, valueBrandIds: valueBrandIds, valueClosureEnv: valueClosureEnv, valueIsCapturingClosure: valueIsCapturingClosure, valueWitnessIds: ValueIdArray.create(), returnLine: 0, declaredAt: SourcePosition.synthesized(), needsGreenThreadStackGuard: needsGreenThreadStackGuard)
		let funcIndex = module.funcCount() as FuncIndex
		module.pushFunc(func)

		return Self{module: module, project: project, funcIndex: funcIndex, func: func, block: module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry"), valueTags: valueTags, valueNameIds: valueNameIds, valueBrandIds: valueBrandIds, valueClosureEnv: valueClosureEnv, valueIsCapturingClosure: valueIsCapturingClosure}
	end 'open'

	// Allocate a fresh ValueId AND record its type, in lockstep — `Parser.mintValue`'s twin over the
	// columns this function owns. The id IS the column height before the push, which is the same dense
	// numbering `LowerMaxonToStd` resumes from.
	//
	// It takes a TAG rather than a `MaxonType` for `Parser.mintValue`'s reason and one of its own: a
	// runtime drop's result is `void`, which is a `ValueTypeTag` and deliberately NOT a `MaxonType` (see
	// MaxonDialect — void names no value).
	export function mint(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns ValueId
		let id = self.valueTags.count()
		self.valueTags.push(tag)
		self.valueNameIds.push(self.projectScopedNameId(tag, nameId: nameId))
		self.valueBrandIds.push(UnnamedTypeId)
		self.valueClosureEnv.push(0)
		self.valueIsCapturingClosure.push(false)
		return id
	end 'mint'

	// Mint a value whose type is already in hand — decomposing the union rather than constructing one,
	// exactly as `Parser.mintValueOfType` does.
	export function mintOfType(valueType MaxonType) returns ValueId
		return self.mint(maxonTypeTag(valueType), nameId: typeNameIdOf(valueType))
	end 'mintOfType'

	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY NAME ID THIS CARRIER MINTS CROSSES FROM THE SIGNATURE INDEX'S TABLE INTO THE PROJECT'S,
	// HERE, AND ONCE.** These two functions are built from types the INDEX resolved
	// (`ProgramSignatures.globalInitValue` and the const-evaluator arena behind it), and their ops are
	// appended to the MERGED module, whose readers speak `project.typeNames`. `ProgramSignatures`' own rule
	// says what that costs: *"names travel between interners; ids never do"* — an id means whatever name
	// happens to sit at that number in the other table.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **MEASURED ON THIS COMPILER'S OWN LEXER, 62 TIMES OVER.** `var keywordMap = [b"if":
	// KeywordInfo.create(…), …]` synthesizes one `Map.upsert` per pair, and the value argument's id — the
	// index's `KeywordInfo` — read back through the project's table named a completely unrelated type:
	// **`E3005 argument type mismatch for 'value': expected 'KeywordInfo', got 'SpillSlot'`**. Nothing was
	// wrong with the program; the number meant two things. A repro needs the two tables to have DIVERGED,
	// which is why a small one does not show it — the ids coincide until enough types are declared, and a
	// read that is right only while two independent insertion orders agree is a wrong answer waiting.
	//
	// It sits in `mint` rather than at the five emit sites because that is the ONE place a value of this
	// carrier gets its type columns, and a door list is what rots.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **SO A `mint` TAKES AN INDEX-SPACE ID AND `open` TAKES A PROJECT-SPACE ONE, AND THE TWO HALVES OF
	// THIS CARRIER GENUINELY DISAGREE (SV1 wave 4).** `open` pushes `typeNameIdOf(paramType)` RAW, because the
	// same `paramTypes` array is stored on the `IrFunction` for the merged module to read, and the module
	// speaks the project's table. There is no crossing that could satisfy both, so the contract is:
	//
	//   • **`paramTypes` handed to `open` are PROJECT-space.**
	//   • **`nameId` handed to `mint` / `mintOfType` / `emitPositionalCall(resultNameId:)` is INDEX-space**,
	//     and is crossed here.
	//
	// ⚠ It hid inside this file because `__module_init` and `__maxon_global_cleanup` take one `int` parameter
	// between them, so `open`'s half was never exercised with a NAMED type. **It cost the second producer a
	// wrong answer**: `Runtime/ServiceLoop.maxon` resolved its payload types through `project.typeNames` and
	// minted from them, and the double crossing refused a correct program — that file's header carries the
	// reading. A third producer should read this box before it writes a `mint`.
	function projectScopedNameId(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId) returns TypeNameId
		if not tagNamesAnIndexInternedType(tag) 'carriesNoNameToCross'
			return nameId
		end 'carriesNoNameToCross'

		let signatures = try queryProgramSignatures(self.project) otherwise panic("ModuleInit.projectScopedNameId: queryProgramSignatures failed while synthesizing a module initializer — it is computed and memoized before the pipeline runs, so it cannot fail fresh here")
		return self.project.typeNames.intern(signatures.indexSpaceTypeName(maxonTypeOfTag(tag, nameId: nameId)))
	end 'projectScopedNameId'

	// ⭐ **THE ADDRESS OF A MANAGED GLOBAL'S `.data` SLOT** — `globalAddr <label>`, minted as a pointer
	// (`Parser.emitGlobalAddr`'s twin on this carrier). BOTH synthesized functions open every global with
	// it, so it is one method rather than two identical pairs of lines: `__module_init` writes the slot and
	// `__maxon_global_cleanup` reads it, and if those two ever derived the address differently the init
	// would fill one slot while the cleanup dropped another — a wrong answer at run time, on a program the
	// compiler accepted. The OFFSET is not repeated for the same reason: `GlobalSlotOffset` is the one
	// constant both stores and loads spell.
	function emitSlotAddr(global ManagedGlobal) returns ValueId
		let addr = self.mintOfType(MaxonType.integer)
		self.emit(MaxonOp.globalAddr(addr, label: global.label), range: global.range)
		return addr
	end 'emitSlotAddr'

	// Append a body op, recording its span and the origin of the value it defines — the choke point
	// `FileParseArtifact.emitOp` is for parsed code, and it keeps the identical two invariants.
	export function emit(op MaxonOp, range SourceRange)
		let opIndex = self.module.opCount()
		self.module.appendOp(self.block, op: op)
		self.recordOpTables(op, opIndex: opIndex as OpIndex, range: range)
	end 'emit'

	// ⭐ **OPEN A FRESH BLOCK AND CONTINUE EMITTING INTO IT** (spec-port `lazy-static`). Until the lazy-init
	// routine there was nothing here to branch — `__module_init` and `__maxon_global_cleanup` are each one
	// straight line — so this carrier had exactly one block and no way to leave it.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT DOES NOT TERMINATE THE BLOCK IT LEAVES.** A block's terminator is the caller's statement about
	// control flow and there is no default that is right for both a fall-through and a two-way branch;
	// leaving it unset makes a forgotten terminator a loud IR failure rather than a silently invented edge.
	export function openBlock(label ByteArray) returns IrBlock
		let opened = self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: label)
		self.block = opened
		return opened
	end 'openBlock'

	// The block ops are currently landing in — the half `openBlock` cannot give a caller that needs to
	// terminate the block it was ALREADY in (a diamond's head, minted before either successor existed).
	export function currentBlock() returns IrBlock
		return self.block
	end 'currentBlock'

	// Continue emitting into a block already opened — the half `openBlock` cannot express, since a routine
	// with a diamond has to come back to a block it minted earlier.
	export function resumeBlock(target IrBlock)
		self.block = target
	end 'resumeBlock'

	// Set the terminator of a block that is not the current one — the two-way branch a diamond's head needs,
	// written after both successors exist.
	export function terminateBlock(target IrBlock, op MaxonOp, range SourceRange)
		let opIndex = self.module.opCount()
		self.module.setTerminator(target, op: op)
		self.recordOpTables(op, opIndex: opIndex as OpIndex, range: range)
	end 'terminateBlock'

	// Terminate the block ops are CURRENTLY landing in. It is `terminateBlock` over `self.block` and nothing
	// else; it was a byte-identical second copy of those three lines until the §6 review (finding 5), and
	// what must not be written twice is how a terminator joins the op tables.
	export function terminate(op MaxonOp, range SourceRange)
		self.terminateBlock(self.block, op: op, range: range)
	end 'terminate'

	// The span + value-origin recording both emitters share, so a body op and a terminator cannot come to
	// disagree about which tables an op joins.
	function recordOpTables(op MaxonOp, opIndex OpIndex, range SourceRange)
		self.project.opRanges.record(opIndex, range: range)
		match maxonOpResultValue(op) 'result'
			none then break 'result'
			value(id) then self.project.valueOrigins.record(self.funcIndex, valueId: id, opIndex: opIndex)
		end 'result'
	end 'recordOpTables'

	// ⭐ **A `literal`** — an element's number, an `element_size` stride, a null `element_destroy` (P1.7 slice
	// 2b), a fixed element count, or a scalar argument of a top-level factory call. `MaxonType.integer` for
	// all but the last, because the Maxon tier has no pointer or size type: a machine word is an integer
	// here, the same reading `emitSlotAddr` above gives an ADDRESS. A factory argument carries its own folded
	// type instead, which is how a `bool` or a `float` argument reaches the call at the width it was written
	// at rather than as an i64.
	//
	// It does NOT record the value in a constant column, and `Parser.emitLiteralIn` does: that column is the
	// PARSER's constant view, feeding the shift-count rule and `foldConstOperands` over source the user
	// wrote. Nothing here is folded against anything, so there is no view to keep.
	// ⭐⭐ **THE VALUE'S IDENTITY AND THE OP'S STORAGE ARE TWO ANSWERS, AND AN ENUM CASE IS WHERE THEY PART.**
	// `Parser.emitEnumTagLiteral` makes the same split for a body's `Color.red`: the VALUE is `named(Color)`
	// — which is what an argument check compares against a `Color` parameter — while the OP carries
	// `integer`, because a payload-free enum's value IS its i64 tag and `maxonTypeToStdType` panics outright
	// on a `named` (*"must be resolved to a primitive before lowering"*).
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **WRITTEN AS ONE TYPE, A TOP-LEVEL `KeywordInfo.create(TokenKind.if, …)` COULD ONLY BE WRONG ONE
	// WAY OR THE OTHER — and this compiler's own lexer is written that way.** Minting at the op's `integer`
	// gave **`E3005 argument type mismatch for 'kind': expected 'TokenKind', got 'int'`**, once per keyword;
	// carrying `named` onto the op instead panicked the lowering. Both are the same missing distinction.
	export function emitTypedLiteral(value ParsedInt, valueType MaxonType, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let result = self.mintOfType(valueType)
		self.emit(MaxonOp.literal(result, value: value, valueType: literalStorageType(valueType)), range: range)
		return result
	end 'emitTypedLiteral'

	// ⭐ **THE HEAP RECORD A STRUCT-SHAPED LITERAL LIVES IN (W116)** — `Parser.emitBoxAlloc`'s twin on this
	// carrier, and the ONE genuinely new emit primitive this file needed: the size, the trivial destructor and
	// the `__mm_alloc` are one sequence, so a change to what a record COSTS — a header, an alignment, another
	// allocator door — reaches both producers or neither.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PARSER'S DOOR ALSO ENROLS THE BOX AS AN OWNED TEMPORARY (`trackOwnedTemp`), AND THIS ONE MUST
	// NOT.** That enrolment is a STATEMENT's drop obligation, and `__module_init` has no statement: the box
	// this returns is stored straight into a `.data` slot (or into an enclosing box), whose owner then drops it
	// through `__maxon_global_cleanup` or through a destructor cascade. That difference is why the two are
	// twins rather than one function on a shared carrier.
	function emitBoxAlloc(sizeBytes ParsedInt, resultTag ValueTypeTag, resultNameId TypeNameId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		var allocArgs = ValueIdArray.create()
		allocArgs.push(self.emitTypedLiteral(sizeBytes, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range))
		allocArgs.push(self.emitTypedLiteral(TrivialDestructor, valueType: MaxonType.integer, range: range))
		return self.emitPositionalCall(MmAllocName, args: allocArgs, resultTag: resultTag, resultNameId: resultNameId, range: range)
	end 'emitBoxAlloc'

	// A two-operand comparison yielding an i1 — `Parser.emitCompareAgainstLiteral`'s twin on this carrier,
	// minted at the `boolean` tag its `condBranch` consumer expects.
	//
	// ⭐ **SIGNED, AND THE CALLER HAS NO RANGED TYPE TO SAY OTHERWISE.** Every comparison this carrier
	// builds is the lazy-static guard's `initialized == 0` — an EQUALITY over a flag the compiler mints
	// itself, whose two readings are the same instruction. There is no source operand here whose declared
	// range could ask for anything else, so the answer is stated rather than threaded.
	export function emitCompare(lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, op MaxonCmpOp, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let result = self.mint(ValueTypeTag.boolean, nameId: UnnamedTypeId)
		self.emit(MaxonOp.compare(result, lhs: lhs, rhs: rhs, op: op, signedness: IntegerSignedness.signed), range: range)
		return result
	end 'emitCompare'

	// The raw `.text` ADDRESS of a compiler-internal runtime function, as a value — `Parser.emitRuntimeFuncAddr`'s
	// twin on this carrier, for the array element destructor `__managed_create` stamps into `element_destroy@40`.
	//
	// The value is typed `integer` for `emitSlotAddr`'s reason (an address is a machine word, and the Maxon
	// tier has no pointer type) rather than the parser's `function` tag: the parser's result may be BOUND by
	// user code, whose type then has to name the function, while this one is an argument and nothing else.
	function emitRuntimeFuncAddr(callee ByteArray, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let result = self.mintOfType(MaxonType.integer)
		self.emit(MaxonOp.functionRef(result, funcName: callee), range: range)
		return result
	end 'emitRuntimeFuncAddr'

	// ⭐ **A CALL WHOSE ARGUMENTS ARE ALL POSITIONAL** — every runtime entry point (`__str_clone`,
	// `__str_decref`, `__managed_create`, `__managed_push`), because the runtime has no parameter names for a label to
	// match, AND a DECLARED generic's `create()` (W41), because the door that admits one admits no arguments at
	// all (`Parser.evalConstContainerCreate`). It is named for that property rather than for the runtime,
	// because since W41 the runtime is no longer the only thing it serves: a user factory taking ARGUMENTS goes
	// to `emitCall` directly, its labels being what `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerCall` reorders against the signature.
	export function emitPositionalCall(callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, resultTag ValueTypeTag, resultNameId TypeNameId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		return self.emitCall(callee, args: args, argLabels: emptyArgLabels(args), resultTag: resultTag, resultNameId: resultNameId, range: range)
	end 'emitPositionalCall'

	// ⭐ **A POSITIONAL CALL WHOSE RESULT NOBODY READS** — a drop, a runtime step, a mailbox close. It is
	// `emitPositionalCall` with `void`/`UnnamedTypeId` and the result discarded, which is one spelling of a
	// three-part incantation that was written out at every void site: the `_ =`, the `ValueTypeTag.void` and
	// the `UnnamedTypeId` are one fact — *this call answers nothing* — and `Runtime/ServiceLoop.maxon` makes
	// eight more of them.
	export function emitPositionalCallDiscarding(callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, range SourceRange)
		_ = self.emitPositionalCall(callee, args: args, resultTag: ValueTypeTag.void, resultNameId: UnnamedTypeId, range: range)
	end 'emitPositionalCallDiscarding'

	// ⭐ **CALL A FUNCTION THE AUTHOR DECLARED — a top-level factory** (spec-port `map-struct-bytearray`), and
	// the op every runtime call above is built from. `argLabels` is the only thing that differs between the
	// two: the runtime has no parameter names for a label to match, while a user factory may be called
	// `create(a, b: c)` and `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerCall` reorders against its declared signature.
	//
	// `CalleeMint.notMinted` because both halves of the name are the AUTHOR'S — the type is one they declared
	// and the method is one they wrote — which is what the reserved-callee check needs to know to leave a
	// `__`-prefixed runtime name alone and hold a user name to the rule.
	//
	// `argDefaultSlots` is empty and that is exact: this walk passes every argument the source wrote and
	// synthesizes none, so there is no supplied default whose declaration slot `slotCallArgs` would need.
	function emitCall(callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, argLabels ByteArrayArray, resultTag ValueTypeTag, resultNameId TypeNameId, range SourceRange) returns ValueId
		let result = self.mint(resultTag, nameId: resultNameId)
		self.emit(MaxonOp.call(result, callee: callee, args: args, argLabels: argLabels, argRanges: uniformArgRanges(args, range: range), argImmutableNames: NoImmutableArgNames, argDefaultSlots: NoDefaultArgSlots, mint: CalleeMint.notMinted), range: range)
		return result
	end 'emitCall'
end 'SynthesizedFunction'
